The energy released by converting a mass \(m\) into energy is  

\[
E = mc^{2}
\]

where \(c = 3.00\times10^{8}\ \text{m/s}\).

The problem asks for the number of protons whose total mass, when converted to energy, yields \(63.1\ \text{J}\).

---

### 1.  Mass that corresponds to \(63.1\ \text{J}\)

\[
m = \frac{E}{c^{2}} = 
\frac{63.1\ \text{J}}{(3.00\times10^{8}\ \text{m/s})^{2}}
= \frac{63.1}{9.00\times10^{16}}\ \text{kg}
\approx 7.01\times10^{-16}\ \text{kg}
\]

---

### 2.  Mass of a single proton  

The mass of a proton is  

\[
m_p = 1.6726\times10^{-27}\ \text{kg}
\]

---

### 3.  Number of protons  

\[
N = \frac{m}{m_p}
   = \frac{7.01\times10^{-16}\ \text{kg}}
          {1.6726\times10^{-27}\ \text{kg}}
   \approx 4.19\times10^{11}
\]

Thus roughly \(4.2\times10^{11}\) protons are required.

---

