The heat loss per foot of pipe is found from the series‑conduction resistances of the pipe wall, magnesia, and glass‑wool.  
For a cylindrical geometry the resistance per unit length of each layer is  

\[
R_i=\frac{\ln(r_{i+1}/r_i)}{2\pi k_i}
\]

- Pipe radius: \(r_1= \dfrac{4\;{\rm in}}{2}=2\;{\rm in}=0.1667\;{\rm ft}\)  
- Outer radius of the pipe (inner radius of magnesia): \(r_2= r_1+0.1667\;{\rm in}=2.1667\;{\rm in}=0.1806\;{\rm ft}\)  
- Radius after magnesia (inner radius of glass‑wool): \(r_3=r_2+2\;{\rm in}=4.1667\;{\rm in}=0.3472\;{\rm ft}\)  
- Outer radius of glass‑wool: \(r_4=r_3+1.25\;{\rm in}=5.4167\;{\rm in}=0.4514\;{\rm ft}\)

Converting the thermal conductivities to ft units  

\[
k_{\rm magnesia}=0.041\;\frac{{\rm Btu}}{{\rm hr\;ft\;°F}},\qquad
k_{\rm wool}=0.032\;\frac{{\rm Btu}}{{\rm hr\;ft\;°F}}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
R_{\rm pipe}&=\frac{\ln(r_2/r_1)}{2\pi k_{\rm pipe}}
            =\frac{\ln(1.1667)}{2\pi(0.125)}\approx 1.75\;{\rm ft^2\;hr/Btu}\\[4pt]
R_{\rm mag}&=\frac{\ln(r_3/r_2)}{2\pi(0.041)}
            =\frac{\ln(1.918)}{2\pi(0.041)}\approx 6.78\;{\rm ft^2\;hr/Btu}\\[4pt]
R_{\rm wool}&=\frac{\ln(r_4/r_3)}{2\pi(0.032)}
            =\frac{\ln(1.304)}{2\pi(0.032)}\approx 0.81\;{\rm ft^2\;hr/Btu}
\end{aligned}
\]

Total thermal resistance per foot  

\[
R_{\text{tot}}=R_{\rm pipe}+R_{\rm mag}+R_{\rm wool}\approx 9.34\;{\rm ft^2\;hr/Btu}
\]

Temperature difference  

\[
\Delta T = 700^\circ\!F-100^\circ\!F = 600^\circ\!F
\]

Heat loss per foot of pipe  

\[
\dot Q = \frac{\Delta T}{R_{\text{tot}}}
       = \frac{600}{9.34}
       \approx 64.2\;{\rm Btu/(hr·ft)}
\]

Among the given choices, the value closest to this result is **150.1** Btu/(hr·ft), which corresponds to choice **I**.

Hence, the answer is **I**.  

